<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magadha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magadha was an ancient kingdom in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>. It was the power base from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> expanded across most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Centuries later it was again the core region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magadha.jpg" alt="Magadha" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahajanapadas_(c._500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Magadha was a region and kingdom of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>, one of the sixteen Mahajanapadas of the period of the second urbanisation. Before it expanded, its territory was bounded on the north, west and east by the Ganga, Son and Campa rivers, with the eastern spurs of the Vindhya mountains to the south, and corresponded to the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> and Gaya districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. Its first capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>, now Rajgir, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, now Patna. A wider Greater Magadha, covering neighbouring parts of the eastern Gangetic plain, had a distinct culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>, composed about 1200 to 900 BCE, lists the Magadhas among non-Vedic tribes outside the Kuru-Panchala cultural sphere, and according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> the Brihadratha dynasty was the first to rule. Information on early rulers is scarce; the main sources are the Buddhist Pali Canon, the Jain Agamas and the Puranas, all written long after the events. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryanka-dynasty">Haryanka dynasty</a> is said to have ruled for some 130 years, with dates that are uncertain and could be significantly later. Its notable kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>, appear in Buddhist and Jain literature as contemporaries of the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a>. Magadha grew to include most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> by conquering the Vajjika League and Anga.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda">Mahapadma Nanda</a> took the throne and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a>, which conquered much of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 345 to about 322 BCE. Chandragupta Maurya overthrew it and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, which lasted from about 322 to 185 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanva-dynasty">Kanva dynasty</a> followed, then rule from outside and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, from about 240 to about 579, centred in Magadha. The Later Guptas and the Maukharis ruled parts of the region afterwards, and from the 11th to the late 13th century Buddhist kings known as the Pithipatis called themselves Lords of Magadha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Magadha was a centre of the sramana movements, which rejected Vedic authority and gave rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivika</a>. The Buddha lived much of his life in Magadha, attained enlightenment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, and the first Buddhist council was held at Rajgriha. Early Magadhan kings supported Buddhism and Jainism. Its language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadhi-prakrit">Magadhi Prakrit</a>, later evolved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magahi">Magahi</a>, Maithili and Bhojpuri, and the Pali language has traditionally been identified with Magadhi. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> lie in the region: the Mahabodhi Temple at Bodh Gaya and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a> monastery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magadha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty's founder, Sri Gupta, established a modest kingdom in or near <b>Magadha</b> around 240 to 280 CE, but imperial expansion began under Chandragupta I, who ascended around 320 CE and is conventionally treated as the true founder.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Magadha</b> lay south of the Ganges in what is now southern Bihar, a land of alluvial plains bounded by the river Son to the west and the forested hills of the Chota Nagpur plateau to the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maurya Empire was founded around 321 BCE by Chandragupta Maurya, who overthrew the Nanda dynasty of <b>Magadha</b> and took advantage of the political vacuum left in north-western India after Alexander the Great's campaigns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late ninth century the Pratiharas under Mihira Bhoja and Mahendrapala I pushed the Palas back and occupied <b>Magadha</b> for a time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Because the Puranas also credit the Andhras with ending the Kanva dynasty of <b>Magadha</b>, and because the earliest inscriptions and coins are hard to date, scholars place Simuka anywhere between the late third and the first century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>All agree that he lived and taught in the Ganges plain of north-east India, in the kingdoms of <b>Magadha</b> and Kosala, at a time of growing cities, new states and lively debate among wandering ascetics about the nature of the self and the way to release from rebirth.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
